\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Taper-section magnification}: factor = cosecant of sectioning angle; 57\,$^\circ$ 43' angle gives 10$\times$ magnification.
  \item \textbf{Unetched vs etched examination}: polished unetched specimens give a clearer picture of fracture extent; inclusions and hard precipitates are more visible unetched; grain boundaries may be confused with cracks when etched first.
  \item \textbf{Fracture-surface etching}: reveals underlying microstructure or produces etch pits indicating dislocation density and crystallographic orientation; SEM gives better results than light microscopy.
  \item \textbf{Differential interference contrast (DIC)}: dark blue-green image with full Wollaston prism insertion shows slip progression and microcrack formation vividly.
  \item \textbf{Selective-area electropolishing}: enables simultaneous study of fracture-surface topography and underlying microstructure (applied to Inconel 718) \textit{(pp.\,189,\,235)}.
\end{itemize}

